1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,404
This tutorial follows one tenancy
contract's money through

2
00:00:03,404 --> 00:00:06,382
Miftah's books, from posting to the
reports.

3
00:00:06,382 --> 00:00:10,212
As the Accountant, open Omar Haddad's
contract for unit

4
00:00:10,212 --> 00:00:13,615
R-101 at Palm Ridge Residences: a 5,000
security

5
00:00:13,615 --> 00:00:17,019
deposit, and 72,000 in rent for the year.

6
00:00:17,019 --> 00:00:18,721
Select View posting journal.

7
00:00:18,721 --> 00:00:21,699
Posting wrote one tenancy contract
journal, TCO.

8
00:00:21,699 --> 00:00:25,103
It debits Rent Receivable with 5,000 and
72,000:

9
00:00:25,103 --> 00:00:28,082
the 77,000 Omar has agreed to pay.

10
00:00:28,082 --> 00:00:31,911
It credits Security Deposit with 5,000,
because the deposit

11
00:00:31,911 --> 00:00:35,315
is held for Omar and is not income.

12
00:00:35,315 --> 00:00:39,144
And it credits Advance Rent with 72,000,
because the

13
00:00:39,144 --> 00:00:42,548
rent is billed now but not yet earned.

14
00:00:42,548 --> 00:00:45,952
Back on the contract, open the Cheques
tab.

15
00:00:45,952 --> 00:00:48,505
Omar handed over five post-dated cheques.

16
00:00:48,505 --> 00:00:51,909
Journal Vouchers lists every journal the
contract wrote.

17
00:00:51,909 --> 00:00:55,738
After the TCO come five post-dated cheque
journals, PDR.

18
00:00:55,738 --> 00:00:59,993
In the Rent Receivable ledger below, each
one credits Rent

19
00:00:59,993 --> 00:01:04,247
Receivable against PDC Receivable, and the
balance falls to zero:

20
00:01:04,247 --> 00:01:08,502
with the cheques in hand, Omar owes
nothing on paper.

21
00:01:08,502 --> 00:01:10,630
Cheque 110102 cleared in January.

22
00:01:10,630 --> 00:01:12,757
Banking a cheque writes nothing.

23
00:01:12,757 --> 00:01:17,437
Clearing writes a receipt journal, CRT: it
debits the Emirates Islamic

24
00:01:17,437 --> 00:01:22,118
account for Palm Ridge Residences with
18,000, and credits PDC Receivable.

25
00:01:22,118 --> 00:01:26,372
The money is the company's when the bank
pays it.

26
00:01:26,372 --> 00:01:28,074
Now open Revenue recognition.

27
00:01:28,074 --> 00:01:32,755
The rent is spread over the term by the
day: October's

28
00:01:32,755 --> 00:01:37,009
31 days earn 6,115.07, and November's 30
days earn 5,917.81.

29
00:01:37,009 --> 00:01:41,690
October's journal debits Advance Rent and
credits Rental Income with 6,115.07.

30
00:01:41,690 --> 00:01:45,944
Income is earned day by day, whenever the
cash arrives.

31
00:01:45,944 --> 00:01:48,497
Next, a cheque that went wrong.

32
00:01:48,497 --> 00:01:51,476
Open the Tenant Ledger for Layla Nasser.

33
00:01:51,476 --> 00:01:55,305
Her rent receivable was at zero until the
bank

34
00:01:55,305 --> 00:01:59,134
returned cheque 220102 in February, and
15,000 came back.

35
00:01:59,134 --> 00:02:02,538
Two replacement cheques of 7,500 closed it
again.

36
00:02:02,538 --> 00:02:07,644
Open the return journal, CBR: it debits
Rent Receivable and credits PDC

37
00:02:07,644 --> 00:02:12,324
Receivable with 15,000, so the debt is
back on the tenant.

38
00:02:12,324 --> 00:02:15,303
In the Trial Balance, the grand total

39
00:02:15,303 --> 00:02:17,855
of the debits equals the credits.

40
00:02:17,855 --> 00:02:20,834
Profit and Loss shows the rental income

41
00:02:20,834 --> 00:02:23,812
earned in August, not the cash collected.

42
00:02:23,812 --> 00:02:27,216
One ledger feeds every one of these
screens.
